<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tumbuka-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tumbuka language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chitumbuka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tumbuka, or Chitumbuka, is a Bantu language spoken in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. In the 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbelwa">Mbelwa</a>, who had conquered the Tumbuka, gradually adopted it in place of their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> language. Scottish missionaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livingstonia">Livingstonia</a> mission used it for schooling and Bible translation, and it is the main language of northern Malawi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tumbuka-language.jpg" alt="Tumbuka language" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tumbuka Arch · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Tumbuka_young_woman_performing_Vimbuza_Dance_of_the_Tumbuka_people.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
